# Part 2 task set: single-defect code review.
#
# 15 tasks, 5 easy / 5 moderate / 5 hard. Every snippet is invented for this
# exercise; none is drawn from any real codebase.
#
# The gradient is how much state a reader must hold to see the defect: one line
# (easy), two related lines (moderate), or an execution-order / lifetime
# argument (hard). Each hard task also carries a DECOY -- a plausible wrong
# answer named in the expectation -- so a confident near-miss is scored as a
# miss rather than as partial credit.
{"id": "e01_offbyone", "difficulty": "easy", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and give the corrected line.\n\n```python\ndef last_n(items, n):\n    # return the final n items, in order\n    return items[len(items) - n - 1:]\n```", "expectation": "Identifies the off-by-one in the slice start: it should be items[len(items)-n:] (or items[-n:]), because subtracting an extra 1 returns n+1 items."}
{"id": "e02_operator", "difficulty": "easy", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and give the corrected line.\n\n```python\ndef is_expired(age_seconds, ttl_seconds):\n    # an entry is expired once it is older than its ttl\n    return age_seconds < ttl_seconds\n```", "expectation": "Identifies the inverted comparison: it should be age_seconds > ttl_seconds (or >=). As written it reports fresh entries as expired and vice versa."}
{"id": "e03_missing_return", "difficulty": "easy", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and give the corrected line.\n\n```python\ndef normalise(name):\n    if name is None:\n        return \"\"\n    name.strip().lower()\n```", "expectation": "Identifies the missing return on the final line: it should be return name.strip().lower(). As written the function returns None."}
{"id": "e04_shadowed_sum", "difficulty": "easy", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and give the corrected line.\n\n```python\ndef total_bytes(chunks):\n    sum = 0\n    for c in chunks:\n        sum += len(c)\n    return sum(chunks)\n```", "expectation": "Identifies that the final line calls sum(chunks) instead of returning the accumulated variable; it should be return sum. Calling the shadowed name raises TypeError because sum is now an int."}
{"id": "e05_int_division", "difficulty": "easy", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and give the corrected line.\n\n```python\ndef average_latency_ms(total_ms, count):\n    if count == 0:\n        return 0.0\n    return total_ms // count\n```", "expectation": "Identifies the floor division: it should be total_ms / count. Integer division discards the fractional part of the average."}
{"id": "m01_mutate_during_iter", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and explain how to fix it.\n\n```python\ndef drop_expired(cache, now):\n    for key, entry in cache.items():\n        if entry.expires_at <= now:\n            del cache[key]\n    return cache\n```", "expectation": "Identifies mutation of the dict while iterating over it, which raises RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration. Fix is to iterate over a snapshot, e.g. list(cache.items()), or build a new dict."}
{"id": "m02_mutable_default", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and explain how to fix it.\n\n```python\ndef collect(item, seen=[]):\n    seen.append(item)\n    return seen\n```", "expectation": "Identifies the mutable default argument: the list is created once at definition time and shared across all calls, so results accumulate between unrelated calls. Fix is seen=None with seen = [] inside the body."}
{"id": "m03_shallow_copy", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and explain how to fix it.\n\n```python\ndef with_extra_header(request, key, value):\n    copy = dict(request)\n    copy[\"headers\"][key] = value\n    return copy\n```", "expectation": "Identifies that dict(request) is a shallow copy, so the nested headers dict is shared and the caller's original request is mutated. Fix is to copy the nested dict too, e.g. copy['headers'] = {**request['headers'], key: value}, or use copy.deepcopy."}
{"id": "m04_swallowed_error", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and explain why it matters.\n\n```python\ndef load_all(paths, parse):\n    results = []\n    for p in paths:\n        try:\n            results.append(parse(p))\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    return results\n```", "expectation": "Identifies that the bare except swallows every failure silently, so the caller receives a short list indistinguishable from a genuinely short input and cannot tell that files failed to parse. Fix is to log or collect the errors, or narrow and re-raise."}
{"id": "m05_float_equality", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and explain how to fix it.\n\n```python\ndef is_fully_spent(spent, budget):\n    return spent == budget\n```", "expectation": "Identifies exact float equality: accumulated floating point spend will essentially never equal the budget exactly, so this returns False when the budget is effectively exhausted. Fix is a tolerance comparison, e.g. spent >= budget - epsilon, or use integers/Decimal."}
{"id": "h01_late_binding", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and give the corrected code.\n\n```python\ndef make_validators(limits):\n    validators = []\n    for limit in limits:\n        validators.append(lambda v: v <= limit)\n    return validators\n```", "expectation": "Identifies the late-binding closure: every lambda captures the variable limit, not its value, so after the loop all validators compare against the LAST limit. Fix is a default argument, lambda v, limit=limit: v <= limit, or functools.partial. Answers that only say 'use a list comprehension' or blame the mutable list do NOT identify the defect."}
{"id": "h02_check_then_act", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and explain how to fix it.\n\n```python\ndef reserve(pool, node_id, amount):\n    # pool.remaining() and pool.take() are each individually thread-safe\n    if pool.remaining() >= amount:\n        pool.take(amount)\n        return True\n    return False\n```", "expectation": "Identifies the check-then-act race (TOCTOU): between remaining() and take() another thread can take the capacity, so two callers can both pass the check and overdraw the pool. Individually thread-safe calls do not compose. Fix is to hold a lock across both, or use an atomic compare-and-take. Answers that say the individual calls need locking have MISSED it."}
{"id": "h03_binary_search", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "This function has exactly one defect. Name the defect and give the corrected line.\n\n```python\ndef first_ge(sorted_values, target):\n    # index of the first value >= target, or len(sorted_values)\n    lo, hi = 0, len(sorted_values) - 1\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if sorted_values[mid] < target:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return lo\n```", "expectation": "Identifies that hi is initialised to len-1 rather than len, so when every value is < target the function returns len-1 instead of the documented len. Fix is hi = len(sorted_values). Answers that claim an infinite loop, or that mid should be lo + (hi-lo)//2 for overflow, have NOT found the defect."}
{"id": "h04_retry_budget", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "This function has exactly one defect relating to the deadline. Name it and explain how to fix it.\n\n```python\ndef call_with_retries(send, payload, attempts=3, deadline=None):\n    last = None\n    for _ in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            return send(payload, timeout=deadline)\n        except TimeoutError as e:\n            last = e\n    raise last\n```", "expectation": "Identifies that the same absolute deadline (or full timeout) is passed to every attempt rather than being decremented by time already elapsed, so the total wall time is up to attempts x deadline instead of bounded by deadline. Fix is to track remaining time and pass the shrinking budget, aborting when it reaches zero. Answers about missing backoff or catching too-narrow an exception have NOT found the stated defect."}
{"id": "h05_partial_write", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "This function claims to write atomically but has exactly one defect that can still leave the destination holding truncated data after a crash. Name it and explain the fix.\n\n```python\ndef save_atomic(path, data, tmp_suffix=\".tmp\"):\n    tmp = path + tmp_suffix\n    with open(tmp, \"w\") as f:\n        f.write(data)\n    os.replace(tmp, path)\n```", "expectation": "Identifies that the file is not flushed and fsynced before os.replace, so the rename can be durable while the data is still in the OS buffer, leaving an empty or truncated file after a power loss. Fix is f.flush() then os.fsync(f.fileno()) inside the with block (ideally also fsyncing the directory). Answers that only say 'os.replace is atomic so this is fine', or that flag the missing import of os, have NOT found the defect."}
